      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Here’s a concise overview of what water is, how it’s structured, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n## 1. Chemical Identity\n- **Formula:** **H₂O** – each molecule consists of two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom.\n- **Molecular weight:** ≈ 18.015 g/mol.\n- **Structure:** Bent (angular) shape with a bond angle of about 104.5°, giving the molecule a permanent dipole (partial negative charge on oxygen, partial positive on hydrogens).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Physical States\n| State | Typical Conditions | Key Characteristics |\n|-------|--------------------|----------------------|\n| **Solid (Ice)** | ≤ 0 °C (32 °F) at 1 atm | Crystalline lattice, less dense than liquid water (hence ice floats). |\n| **Liquid** | 0 °C – 100 °C (32 °F – 212 °F) at 1 atm | Highly cohesive, high surface tension, excellent solvent. |\n| **Gas (Steam/Vapor)** | ≥ 100 °C (212 °F) at 1 atm | Low density, expands to fill any container. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Key Physical & Chemical Properties\n- **Polarity:** Enables strong hydrogen bonding between molecules, leading to high boiling point, high specific heat, and high surface tension.\n- **Specific heat capacity:** ≈ 4.18 J·g⁻¹·K⁻¹ (water can absorb a lot of heat without large temperature changes).\n- **Heat of vaporization:** ≈ 2260 J·g⁻¹ (energy needed to turn liquid water into vapor).\n- **Density:** ≈ 1 g·cm⁻³ at 4 °C (the temperature of maximum density).\n- **pH of pure water:** ≈ 7 (neutral), though it can act as a very weak acid or base (auto‑ionization: 2 H₂O ⇌ H₃O⁺ + OH⁻).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Role as a Solvent\n- **“Universal solvent”**: Because of its polarity and ability to form hydrogen bonds, water dissolves a huge variety of ionic and polar substances (salts, sugars, gases, many organic molecules). This makes it the medium for most biochemical reactions.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Biological Importance\n- **Cellular environment:** Cytoplasm and extracellular fluids are > 90 % water.\n- **Metabolism:** Acts as a reactant, product, and medium for enzymatic reactions; involved in hydrolysis, photosynthesis, respiration, etc.\n- **Temperature regulation:** Through sweating, respiration, and water circulation, organisms maintain stable internal temperatures.\n- **Transport:** Blood, sap, and plant xylem/phloem rely on water to move nutrients, gases, and waste.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Environmental Significance\n- **Hydrologic cycle:** Evaporation → condensation → precipitation → runoff → infiltration → back to oceans.\n- **Climate regulator:** High heat capacity buffers temperature swings; water vapor is a potent greenhouse gas.\n- **Ecosystems:** Aquatic habitats (rivers, lakes, oceans) support diverse life forms; water availability shapes terrestrial ecosystems.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Everyday Examples\n- **Drinking water:** Provides essential hydration and minerals.\n- **Cooking:** Transfers heat efficiently; dissolves flavors.\n- **Cleaning:** Solvent properties remove dirt and oils.\n- **Industrial uses:** Coolants, solvents, feedstock for chemicals (e.g., hydrogen production via electrolysis).\n\n---\n\n### Quick Summary\nWater is a polar, hydrogen‑bonding molecule (H₂O) that exists naturally as ice, liquid, and vapor. Its unique physical properties—high heat capacity, surface tension, and solvent power—make it indispensable for chemistry, biology, climate, and everyday life."
